-- NEGATIVE fixture for the rule-date interval table (spec §15.3, R10).
--
-- Two arms on the SAME day. The second is dead under L4's first-match reading,
-- and the derived interval `[2023-01-01..2023-01-01)` would be empty -- which
-- is isomorphic to the L4, and also un-analysable and almost certainly not what
-- the drafter meant. Duplicates are therefore caught by the same
-- strictly-descending predicate as a misordering, giving one rule and one
-- message shape for both defects.
--
-- NEVER added to `goldenSubjects`. Asserted from jl4/tests/DmnExport.hs.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `duplicate dated chain`

TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

GIVETH A DATE
`the 2023 rate change` MEANS Date 1 1 2023

GIVETH A DATE
`the other 2023 change` MEANS Date 1 1 2023

GIVETH A DATE
`GST commenced` MEANS Date 1 4 1994

ASSUME `no GST rate exists before commencement` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN change IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rules in force include` change MEANS
    `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST change

GIVETH A NUMBER
`GST rate percent` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rules in force include` `the 2023 rate change`  THEN 8
           IF ^                            `the other 2023 change` THEN 7
           IF ^                            `GST commenced`         THEN 3
           OTHERWISE `no GST rate exists before commencement`
